Finds the largest font scale at which no text of a ggplot (or of a
patchwork assembly of ggplots) overlaps another text or leaves the canvas,
once the figure is drawn at \code{width} x \code{height}, and returns the plot with
its text resized by that scale.

Text sizes in ggplot2 are absolute (points), so whether labels overlap
depends on the final figure size. \code{resize_fonts()} draws the figure
off-screen at that size, measures the box of every label (rotated labels
included), and searches the scale by bisection between \code{min_scale} and
\code{max_scale}. Inside a knitr or Quarto chunk, \code{width} and \code{height} default
to the chunk's \code{fig-width} and \code{fig-height}.
}
\section{Limitations}{

\itemize{
\item Only text drawn as plain grid text is measured: rich text from
'ggtext' or 'marquee' is resized but not checked for overlaps.
\item A single scale is applied to every selected category, which keeps the
relative hierarchy of the text sizes; one crowded axis therefore
shrinks all the selected text.
\item When some text still overlaps at \code{min_scale} (e.g. two labels at the
same position), a warning is raised and the plot is returned at
\code{min_scale}. Consider \code{\link[ggplot2:guide_axis]{ggplot2::guide_axis()}} (\code{n.dodge}, \code{angle}) or
wrapping labels instead.
\item Each step draws the whole figure, so large plots take a few seconds.
}
}

